t into um hospitality yeah i  went straight into the kitchen and when  i went into the kitchen i was surrounded  by by people who i would never have met  or come into contact unless i was  working in the kitchen and it was such a  great eye-opener and they were such  decent nice people but it was you you  just learn how to get on with people and  i think when i at that stage i was a  very shy guy so  it was it was good to learn and so at  the time what was your first business so  did you think actually i need to i need  to do things i need to be an  entrepreneur because i set up over the  top restaurant which was  really bad so why were they bad well it  was the food was bad the decor was bad  the service was bad  and you know i i owe it to my friends  who came  regularly to support me  and they had to eat quite a lot of  horrible food but were you down in the  dumps when you realized or did you take  it well i don't know i i felt i was  learning all the time i was learning if  i did this differently would would that  work and would if i tweet that and have  i change that so then i opened cafe  boehm in 92 and then sewer house opened  in 95. so what's your biggest mistake  that you learned from  i the biggest mistake was not to realize  that any of it was a mistake but to  learn by it  and  and and just that it was just a journey  and it was progression and i think i  still do that every day so you've had so  how since 1993 a lot has happened since  then we've become more digitized we had  covered what was the hardest day in this  journey  i think it's been lots of hard days i  think there's been lots of days where  we've run out of money  um  and in the early days and you had to  figure out your way through that  um  and  you know i care deeply about our members  and our  our people who work for us so  you know when anything happens to them  that's a hard day in in those early days  when you ran out of money  do you i mean what do you do do you  swear do you kind of stay positive or  how well i swear to myself  and and and  but you learn never to do that again you  learn again  how  do i  figure out how that doesn't happen are  you always positive are there days where  you just think no i think i think there  are days when i'm i'm challenging you  know i'm challenging to the team and if  you're not  if you always look at everything  you know that it's great then you're not  going to then challenge them to try and  make it better so there's a bit of both  you know in the next three years we're  going to be at 85 houses globally you  know it's all about teamwork it's all  about a culture it's all about  people aiming to make the life of a  member better do you worry about  over-expansion like as an entrepreneur i  guess you choose what city you want to  open in  well  i i i  you know ever since we went from one to  two people went oh  you know it's gonna change and actually  i think  what we found is  the the more global we've become the  more interesting a place we are to come  and work and  i think  we never take anything for granted we  always go into a new city nervous and  thinking that we have to make it has to  work locally if it doesn't work locally  it never works globally what's your  biggest challenge every time you open a  house is it location is it staff  shortages is it  i mean talking big picture it's it's  it's it's  it's making sure you get to the right  right city into the right location and i  think we have a cities with our houses  so we already have members in those  cities who  care about us so they guide us to the  right area of the city and they guide us  to the right building so  and and and staffing yes we got a  short-term  problem with staffing at the moment like  everyone does it doesn't matter if it's  hospitality it doesn't mean  whoever you are and i think you just  have to just get better at employing  people you have to be  go we've come with the times you've got  to you've got to you've got to